module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.DiscreteValuationRing.Basic
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.ClosedImmersion
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.FiniteType
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.Grp.Basic
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceFibreProductsEtaleCovers
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.LocallySeparatedGroupSpaceScheme
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceFpqcSheaf

/-! # Schematicity of separated group algebraic spaces over a trait

Source: Raynaud, Specialisation du foncteur de Picard, Theoreme 3.3.1, printed p.40;
Anantharaman, Schemas en groupes, espaces homogenes et espaces algebriques sur une base
de dimension 1, Theoreme 4.B and section 4.2
(blueprint: lem-sfb-trait-group-space-schematicity).

The group-valued functor encodes the multiplication, identity and inverse, naturally in
scheme tests. Its underlying presheaf is identified with the given algebraic space.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace

/-- A separated group algebraic space locally of finite type over a DVR is a scheme.
The group may be noncommutative, and the space need not be flat or quasi-compact.
Source: Raynaud, Theoreme 3.3.1, printed p.40, specialized to the spectrum of a DVR;
Anantharaman, Theoreme 4.B, section 4.2
(blueprint: lem-sfb-trait-group-space-schematicity).

Proof:
1. Choose a uniformizer pi and residue field k. Base change the group laws and an etale
   atlas to k, using [lem-trf-space-fibre-products-etale-covers]. Separatedness persists
   by [lem-sfb-space-separated-fp-composition], and the charts remain locally of finite
   type by [lem-weil-pbc-lft-base-change]. A closed diagonal is an immersion, so
   [lem-sfb-locally-separated-group-space-scheme] represents the special fibre.
2. For n >= 1 base change to Spec(R/(pi^n)). The special fibre is a thickening in this
   space: on charts its ideal is generated by pi, whose nth power vanishes. Every prime
   contains it, so the induced map on point spaces is a homeomorphism. Apply
   [lem-sfb-thickening-of-scheme-space] to represent every infinitesimal neighbourhood.
3. Apply [lem-sfb-space-trait-flat-group-closure]. The full generic-fibre closure C is a
   closed group subspace, defined on an affine etale chart Spec B by ker(B -> B[1/pi]).
   It has the same generic fibre and is separated, flat and locally of finite type.
4. By [lem-sfb-flat-group-space-fpqc-scheme-cover], an fpqc base change T -> Spec R
   makes C a separated flat group scheme locally of finite presentation over T.
5. By [lem-a1-algebraic-space-fpqc-sheaf], C is an fpqc sheaf, and its group operations
   make it a sheaf of groups. Apply [lem-sfb-trait-flat-group-fpqc-effectivity] to the
   cover and group scheme from step 4, over the original trait, to represent C.
6. Apply [lem-sfb-trait-space-closure-thickenings-scheme] to X, the full generic closure
   C from steps 3 and 5, and the infinitesimal neighbourhoods from step 2. The closure
   kernel formula and all schematicity hypotheses hold. Thus X is an S-scheme.
No step imposes flatness on X, finite type on its generic fibre, or an affine-neighbourhood
hypothesis. -/
theorem exists_scheme_of_trait_group
    {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [IsDomain R] [IsDiscreteValuationRing R]
    (X : AlgebraicSpace (Spec (.of R)))
    (F : (Over (Spec (.of R)))ᵒᵖ ⥤ GrpCat.{u})
    (e : F ⋙ forget GrpCat ≅ X.obj.obj)
    (hsep : IsRepresentableWith (fun _ _ p ↦ IsClosedImmersion p.left)
      (prod.lift (𝟙 X.obj.obj) (𝟙 X.obj.obj)))
    (hlft : ∀ (U : Over (Spec (.of R))) (a : yoneda.obj U ⟶ X.obj.obj),
      IsEtale a → LocallyOfFiniteType U.hom) :
    ∃ Y : Over (Spec (.of R)), Nonempty (X.obj.obj ≅ yoneda.obj Y) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace
